{
  // Tree cutover check: one grown oak, spruce and cactus each, a cut limb and a felled trunk.
  // Run from OpenGLTest/ with
  //   VOXEL_TIME=147 VOXEL_SCRIPT=visual-examples/trees.json
  // Look for floating wood, gaps between a node box and its tubes, black faces, wrong tiles, a
  // crown still standing after the limb cut and nothing left standing after the base cut.
  //
  // Trees are grown per 64-block cell (TreeBatch: every tree of a cell together, avoiding the
  // earlier trees of the batch and the neighbor cells' solo shapes), so the coordinates below
  // were probed through TreeGenerator.BatchShapeOf / BatchOf, which is what the chunks stamp.
  // Coordinates (default seed, headless probe over TreeGenerator.TryGetTree / BatchShapeOf):
  //   oak    base (-79, 14, -2), 12 tall, 69 wood cells and 278 leaves, crown x -84..-74,
  //          z -8..4; nearest other trunk 9 away; the root and the three cells over it are a full
  //          16 px log (phase 9). Limb: node (-79, 19, -4), 3 px thick, hanging off the 5 px stub
  //          (-79, 19, -3) with two children, (-79, 19, -5) and (-79, 20, -4), so 4 wood cells in
  //          its subtree, all on the -Z side away from its parent. Seen from under the crown at
  //          (-81.13, 17.84, -4.09), 3.2 blocks away, through exactly one leaf cell (-80, 19, -4)
  //          (the ray was checked against every cell box grown and shrunk by 0.03, so no corner
  //          grazes; no wood within one cell of the eye). base_aim's first hit is the root cell.
  //   spruce base (-291, 13, -252), top leaf 11 above it, 35 wood cells, about 130 blocks south of
  //          tree-perf.json's taiga vantage: one of the few spruces of that dense wood standing
  //          clear of its neighbors. Filmed from 14 blocks west at base + 6.5, where every
  //          sampled cell of the tree is seen with no other tree's cell and no terrain on the
  //          sight line; its own conical crown reaches the ground and hides the trunk.
  //   cactus base (601, 17, 1282), 9 above its base, by the desert vantage of tree-perf.json: a
  //          two-armed pitchfork (phase 9) with both arms along x, the west one leaving at
  //          base + 2 for a 3-cell level run and a 4-cell prong (top base + 6), the east one at
  //          base + 4 for 2 cells and a 3-cell prong (top base + 7). Filmed broadside from
  //          11.5 blocks south so both arms read in profile, with nothing within two cells of
  //          the eye and no other cactus on any sight line to its cells.
  // Every vantage was chosen so that no block stands within two cells of the eye and nothing
  // but the subject crosses the sight line; the limb cut is filmed from the -Z side it hangs on.
  // The camera flies the whole time, which implies creative: every `mine` of one frame
  // (`seconds` 0.001) removes exactly the block under the crosshair, so the first one clears the
  // leaf in front of the limb and the second cuts the limb itself. The oak comes first so the
  // cuts happen in chunks that loaded around it, before the long hops to the taiga and desert.
  //
  // Leaf decay (LeafDecayManager): limb_cut_before_decay is shot 0.3 s after the cut, while the
  // subtree topples and the leaves it fed beyond the felling sweep (Chebyshev 2 around its wood)
  // still stand; limb_cut_decayed is shot 4 s later. The decay runs one tick every
  // LeafDecayTickSeconds, re-deriving each scheduled leaf's hydration from the live tips around
  // it, and removes at most LeafDecayMaxRemovalsPerTick tipless leaves (and orphaned thin wood) a
  // tick in a hash-scattered order, so a cut crown speckles away over a couple of seconds. Look
  // for the leaves beyond the sweep gone in limb_cut_decayed, the standing crown untouched, and no
  // floating node boxes left where the limb was. felled_after, 3 s after felled_down, should show
  // nothing standing: every leaf of a felled oak goes down with its wood.
  //
  // Reactive growth (`grow`, World.TryGrowTree -> TreeGrowthWriter): the step snaps its [x, z]
  // to TryGroundHeight + 1, rebuilds the tree rooted in that column from its wood state bytes
  // (or plants a sapling root on the real ground when the column holds none), runs `steps`
  // growth pulses in the live world and writes the new wood and the leaves around the changed
  // tips back. (a) oak_grown grows the cut oak further, filmed from the overview vantage: the
  // cut limb must stay missing (neither regrown nor re-attached), the trunk must not thin, and
  // new tips and leaves should appear elsewhere on the crown. (b) sapling_1..3 plant on the
  // felled oak's column (the fell took the root cell and its root walk, so it is bare ground) and
  // grow it in three stages from the `oak` vantage: look for a rooted tree, no floating wood or
  // leaves. Each grow schedules leaf decay over the box it wrote, so a few leaves the old tips
  // fed may speckle away after it, and every grown column loses its far-LOD canopy until reload
  // (the LOD hook can only remove a canopy bump, not add one).
  //
  // Wide trunks (phase 7): the last block plants a baobab and a stone pine, neither of which
  // has a worldgen block, on open flat forest about 40 blocks south-west of the oak. Both columns
  // were probed headless: the generated ground is level over the 9x9 around each root (grass,
  // dry), no worldgen tree cell stands inside either grown tree's crown box grown by one (re-probed
  // in phase 9 for the wider stone pine), every sight line below crosses only air before the
  // subject, and the grows were
  // replayed with the writer's call counter at 4 and 5 (the four earlier grows of this scenario
  // advance it; a new grow inserted before them reshapes both trees).
  //   baobab     root (-121, 11, 40), planted with 120 steps: 186 wood cells (nodes, shells and
  //              fins), 244 leaves, crown x -126..-116, y 11..26, z 35..45. Circle trunk of
  //              block radius 2 up to root + 2, radius 1 to root + 4, the crown forking at root + 5;
  //              buttress fins on the root level run west to x -124, north to z 37 and south to
  //              z 44. The cut slice is y 12 (root + 1, above the fins): 21 cells, rows z 39..41
  //              five wide (x -123..-119), the outer rows z 38 and z 42 three wide (x -122..-120).
  //   stone pine root (-90, 14, 43), planted with 90 steps (phase 9 shape): 88 wood cells and
  //              451 leaves, an 11-cell bare trunk (top y 24) splitting into four bare limbs,
  //              one per side, that stair-step out and up (limb wood y 22..29), with flat leaf
  //              pads only at their ends (y 25..30); crown x -100..-79, z 33..53, 16 tall. Filmed
  //              from 24 blocks south at y 22, where the whole tree fits the frame; open ground,
  //              no worldgen tree cell inside its crown box grown by one.
  // baobab_grown should show a wide round trunk with stair-stepped beveled corners on its outer
  // shells and the fins resting on the ground; baobab_rim is the close look at the trunk base:
  // each rim must match its outline (aim at it in a manual run), with no hole above or below a
  // beveled shell and full faces where a shell meets the core or another shell. stone_pine_grown
  // shows the wide umbrella crown on its bare trunk. stone_pine_limbs, appended after every
  // grow and cut so the writer's call counter for the two grows above is unchanged, looks up
  // 20 degrees at the trunk from 9 blocks out on the south-east diagonal at y 22.5 (root + 8.5,
  // between lowestBranchHeight and the pads, nothing within two cells of the eye): bare limbs
  // must show under the pads, with the leaves only as caps at the limb ends (18 of the 30 bare
  // limb cells are on a clear sight line).
  // The cut follows the limb idiom: every cell is aimed from 4 blocks west along its own row (eye
  // at the cell's z + 0.5 and y 12.5, yaw 0, pitch 0, inside InteractionReach), so the ray crosses
  // only the row's cells already cut, and one single-frame creative `mine` removes exactly that
  // cell. The three middle rows go first; baobab_partial_cut, filmed from 13 blocks west at slot
  // height (beyond InteractionReach, so no outline) once the debris has settled, must show sky
  // and terrain through the notch and the tree still standing on the two outer rows
  // (shell-to-shell vertical links carry it). Cutting the core alone (the eighth cut) drops just
  // that cell and the two leaves the felling sweep finds within two cells of it. The last of the
  // six outer-row cuts fells everything above the slice (383 blocks), which tips east, away from
  // the eye: baobab_felled_falling should show the whole crown toppling and baobab_felled_down it
  // lying, with the root slice and the fins left standing. Known truncation: the felled mesh is
  // drawn only inside its 16^3 scratch chunk, so a tree wider than that would lose its rim while
  // falling; its drops are intact either way.
  // The grown shots wait 3 s: a chunk meshed for the first time (the air chunks the crowns grow
  // into) fades in over Chunk.MAX_LIFETIME once its asynchronous mesh lands, and a shot taken
  // during that fade shows the crown half see-through and sky-tinted. Natural slime and goblin
  // spawns roam this open forest and may stand in a shot; a mob inside the punch cone of a cut
  // would take the punch instead of the cell, so check the log's mine steps if a cut looks short.
